Then, in this order:
a. Rebuild the code object after point 1, run the unit tests, and **register its upload with the governor at once** (one attempt, honest bytes, no priority change). The queue took two hours last time; the object may wait there while you rehearse. An uploaded object that is never sealed is harmless: if a rehearsal fails, it is retired.
b. The whole-worker pilot rehearsal on C7 (my 0758Z grant covers it), the coverage review, then the fresh full rehearsal (ask me by name with the free-space reading).
c. **No seal before** the pilot rehearsal and the coverage review pass, the upload is confirmed, and my order. Send one RESULT with the family candidate, the proofs and the call-site table.

**Official VSI-Bench direct read (parallel work while C7 waits).** Lane official544 delivered the six-task kit: \`$R/official544/events/20261115T0802Z_RESULT_direct_six_reviewed.md\` (4,008 items; routed types not read; labels kept out of generation; launcher patch and CPU dry run). The first review is \`$R/o544_review/out/REVIEW_VERDICT_OFFICIAL544.md\`. You are the Orchard owner and a different model family from its builder, so you do the second review as part of adoption: read the diff against the kit the first review read, check that each of F1 to F9 is closed, rerun its CPU dry run, and confirm the job prints package versions and compares the frame layout on real pixels before any item is scored. If it holds, register the kit's upload with the governor, prepare the submission packet (one H100, advanced; account rule unchanged), and send me a RESULT with the packet and the projected run time. Submission needs my order. If it does not hold, write the findings into \`$R/official544/inbox/\` as an order to that lane and tell me.
